Here's an optimized .jsx script for InDesign that transforms a spreads document with multiple master pages into a single-page document while keeping the original margins, layout, positioning of the elements, and references to master pages:

```javascript
#target indesign

// Get the active document
var doc = app.activeDocument;

// Create a new document with the same properties as the original
var newDoc = app.documents.add({
    documentPreferences: {
        pageHeight: doc.documentPreferences.pageHeight,
        pageWidth: doc.documentPreferences.pageWidth,
        pageOrientation: doc.documentPreferences.pageOrientation,
        marginPreferences: doc.marginPreferences,
        gridPreferences: doc.gridPreferences
    }
});

// Loop through each page in the original document
for (var i = 1; i <= doc.pages.length; i++) {
    // Get the current page
    var page = doc.pages[i - 1];

    // Create a new page in the new document
    var newPage = newDoc.pages.add();

    // Loop through each item on the current page
    for (var j = 0; j < page.allPageItems.length; j++) {
        // Get the current item
        var item = page.allPageItems[j];

        // Duplicate the item and add it to the new page
        item.duplicate(LocationOptions.at, newPage);
    }

    // Set the master page for the new page
    newPage.appliedMasterPage = page.appliedMasterPage;
}

// Adjust the page size and layout of the new document
newDoc.documentPreferences.pageSize = doc.documentPreferences.pageSize;
newDoc.documentPreferences.pageOrientation = doc.documentPreferences.pageOrientation;
newDoc.documentPreferences.pageHeight = doc.documentPreferences.pageHeight;
newDoc.documentPreferences.pageWidth = doc.documentPreferences.pageWidth;
newDoc.marginPreferences = doc.marginPreferences;
newDoc.gridPreferences = doc.gridPreferences;

// Save the new document
newDoc.save(new File(doc.fullName.parent + "/" + doc.name.replace(".indd", "_single.indd")));
```

Here's how the script works:

1. The script gets the active document and creates a new document with the same properties as the original (page size, orientation, margins, and grid preferences).
2. It then loops through each page in the original document, creates a new page in the new document, and duplicates all the page items from the original page to the new page.
3. The script also sets the master page for each new page to match the original.
4. Finally, the script adjusts the page size and layout of the new document to match the original, and saves the new document with a "_single" suffix.

This script is optimized by using the `allPageItems` property to get all the items on a page, rather than looping through each layer and page item individually. This can significantly improve performance for large documents with many page items.

